  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="show_interaction" outcome-identifier="INTERACTION_VISIBILITY" show-hide="show" class="qti-margin-0">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>In which of these sentences is stratification used correctly?</p>
    <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="RESPONSE" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">The online game sorted players into ranked tiers, from Bronze at the bottom up through Silver, Gold, and Diamond — a clear stratification of the community by skill.</qti-simple-choice>
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The commune achieved perfect stratification, since every member held exactly the same rank and no one stood above anyone else.</qti-simple-choice>
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">The stratification of the club was so flat that it had no levels or ranks whatsoever — just one undivided group of equals.</qti-simple-choice>
      <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The papers were swept into a single random pile with no order or ranking at all, a textbook case of stratification.</qti-simple-choice>
    </qti-choice-interaction>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>

    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-A" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct! Stratification means what this sentence shows it means.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-B" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! The other sentences misuse stratification — wrong strength, wrong situation, or wrong pairing. The correct one is: &quot;The online game sorted players into ranked tiers, from Bronze at the bottom up through Silver, Gold, and Diamond — a clear stratification of the community by skill.&quot;</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-C" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! The other sentences misuse stratification — wrong strength, wrong situation, or wrong pairing. The correct one is: &quot;The online game sorted players into ranked tiers, from Bronze at the bottom up through Silver, Gold, and Diamond — a clear stratification of the community by skill.&quot;</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="FB-D" outcome-identifier="FEEDBACK" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Not quite! The other sentences misuse stratification — wrong strength, wrong situation, or wrong pairing. The correct one is: &quot;The online game sorted players into ranked tiers, from Bronze at the bottom up through Silver, Gold, and Diamond — a clear stratification of the community by skill.&quot;</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
